  "summary": "Crowd-built benchmarks and reference-free pipelines are testing how far computational models can recover binding and splicing from raw data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is computational inference of transcript-level and binding interactions from high-throughput or sequence data, with validation or benchmarking central to each. PCLIPtools statistically identifies high-confidence RBP-RNA interaction sites from PAR-CLIP data using read depth and T-to-C conversions. IRCAS detects alternative splicing events without a genome reference, combining colored de Bruijn graph detection, CNN-based splice site rectification, and GAT-Transformer GNN classification. The IBIS community challenge benchmarks crowd-built models of human transcription factor DNA binding, where deep learning is only slightly ahead of PWMs, which remain strong out of the box. MPKaDB, a membrane protein pKa database predicting ionizable residue protonation states at a given pH, lies at the periphery of transcriptomics. What is new is the reference-free and crowd-benchmarked framing applied to transcript-level and binding inference.",
